Title: Add third_party/winwrap, a Win32 compatibility layer for legacy Windows
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

Groundwork for Windows XP, and as of the AppUserModelID pair below, a fix for a
real Vista crash. Scanning the shipped x64 electron.exe against real system DLLs
gives the size of the problem: 2 symbols missing on Vista SP2, but 28 on XP x64
and 38 on XP SP3 x86 (tools/check_win_imports.py reproduces this). A statically
imported symbol the target OS does not export is fatal at load, before any
process code runs, so none of it can be fixed at a call site.

Vista's two are the TryAcquireSRWLock variants, which nothing in Node or
Electron calls; patches/node/06-node-xp-vista-support.patch handles those by teaching libuv they can be
absent. XP is a different problem. Its list is the synchronisation primitives
themselves -- the whole SRWLOCK family, condition variables, InitOnceExecuteOnce
-- which are used pervasively and have no "skip it" answer. 38 call-site patches
is not a plan, so this is a component instead.

How calls are captured, with no change at any call site: a
__declspec(dllimport) call does not call the function, it loads a pointer from
__imp_<name> and calls through that. Defining __imp_<name> ourselves is enough.
The linker resolves the reference here and never extracts kernel32.lib's member
for it -- no duplicate symbol, because archive members are only pulled to
satisfy still-undefined references. And kernel32.lib is never named in GN: it
arrives through /DEFAULTLIB directives the SDK headers embed in every object,
and those are processed after command-line libraries, so an explicitly linked
winwrap wins without reordering anything.

The symbol is decorated per architecture -- __imp_GetTickCount64 on x64,
__imp__GetTickCount64@0 on x86 -- and the x86 form is not a legal C identifier,
so redirects.cc attaches it with an asm label and keeps the stdcall byte count
beside each name.

Two link modes, is_component_winwrap in third_party/winwrap/winwrap.gni:
static winwrap.lib (default) or shared winwrap.dll. Static is the default
because it ships nothing extra, so there is no separate binary anyone has to
trust -- which is the point of having our own rather than consuming a prebuilt
wrapper DLL we cannot audit. use_winwrap gates it entirely, and defaults off:
the redirects capture every call in the binary, so an ordinary build would get
a CRITICAL_SECTION where it asked for an SRWLOCK, for nothing.

Verified against the packaged toolchain and the real system DLLs, both
architectures: every translation unit compiles against the 10.0.26100.0 SDK;
redirects.cc emits exactly the nine synchronisation symbols with correct x86
byte counts (@0, @4, @16); a consumer calling all nine through the SDK headers,
linked against the real kernel32.Lib, comes out with none of them in its import
table; and that binary scans clean against XP SP3 x86 and XP x64 exports --
winwrap's own imports are all XP-era primitives.

The shared mode is now confirmed on a real 32-bit build rather than in a
harness: out/XP-x86/winwrap.dll exports all ten undecorated names, and
electron.exe from the same build imports winwrap.dll with no remaining
kernel32 import for GetTickCount64, InitOnceExecuteOnce or InitializeSRWLock.
Undecorated alias targets in winwrap.def are therefore fine on x86 -- lld-link
matches winwrap_GetTickCount64 against the decorated _winwrap_GetTickCount64@0
that __stdcall emits -- and the warning that file used to carry about 32-bit
shared builds was wrong.

The shell32 AppUserModelID pair is the one part of this that is not about XP,
and it is reached differently from everything else here. shell32 is
delay-loaded, so a missing export is not fatal at load; it surfaces on the
first call, where Chromium's delay-load failure hook ends in NOTREACHED().
Windows then reports APPCRASH with exception code 0x80000003 and an offset and
nothing else, which is how a one-line gap cost a full diagnostic cycle. The
mechanism is unchanged -- satisfying __imp_<name> here means shell32.lib is
never consulted for that symbol and no delay thunk is generated for it -- and
the library ordering works out for a different reason than it does for
kernel32: winwrap.dll.lib is a link *input*, which the GN toolchain places
ahead of ${libs} in the response file, and shell32.lib is in ${libs}. That is
the same reason the kernel32 redirect holds even though kernel32.lib is itself
named explicitly in that list, contrary to what the /DEFAULTLIB reasoning above
assumes; both paths work, but the input-ordering one is what is actually doing
the work.

Vista has neither taskbar grouping nor jump lists, so there is no AppUserModelID
concept to implement: Set accepts an ID and does nothing with it, and Get
reports that no ID has been set, which is what Windows itself returns for a
process that never called Set. Electron's GetRawAppUserModelID() already
handles that result and derives an ID from the application name instead.

Both still answer like the real functions where the answer is observable. Set
rejects a null ID with E_INVALIDARG rather than claiming success for a call
that could not have meant anything; it does not enforce the documented length
and no-spaces rules, because Windows rejecting those means a caller already
copes, and accepting one here keeps the shim from disagreeing in the direction
nobody tests. Get clears its out parameter, because that same Electron function
declares an uninitialised PWSTR and passes it to CoTaskMemFree()
unconditionally.

winwrap_internal_config drops _WIN32_WINNT to 0x0501 for winwrap's own sources,
via cflags with a -U first because Chromium sets it globally. That is load
bearing rather than cosmetic: it hides the post-XP declarations from this
library while leaving consumers at 0x0A00, so a Vista-or-later call added here
fails to compile instead of becoming a runtime surprise on XP. winwrap still
builds because it needs only the types, which live ungated in winnt.h, and
never calls the functions it stands in for. It is also why app_user_model_id.cc
declares the two shell32 signatures in winwrap.h rather than including
shobjidl.h, which hides them below NTDDI_WIN7.

Everything here is 7-bit ASCII, deliberately. rc.py passes /utf-8 to the
resource compiler only when the .rc itself opens with a UTF-16LE BOM; without
one the compiler rejects any character above 7-bit ASCII -- including one
produced by an escape, so L"\251 ..." for the copyright sign fails just as a
literal would. Making the .rc UTF-16 fixes that but then the patch needs a GIT
binary hunk, and a binary hunk is the one thing in this series that cannot
survive a CRLF-converting checkout: it broke the Windows build server, where
the other fourteen patches applied fine. LEGAL_COPYRIGHT therefore says "(c)",
which keeps this patch pure text and keeps the .rc and its header from having
to agree on an encoding forever.

Linked in through //build/config:common_deps, which BUILD.gn calls out as a
dependency of every executable and shared library. That is the right altitude
for this: the redirect works by satisfying the __imp_<name> slot a call site
loads through, and every final link resolves those independently, so putting it
on one target would leave the others importing kernel32. common_deps is the one
place that covers executables, shared libraries and loadable modules together.
The shared build of winwrap sets no_default_deps to avoid depending on itself
through that same group, and the dep is guarded on is_win as well as
use_winwrap: is_win follows current_os, not target_os, so without it a cross
build from Linux would hand winwrap to the host tools built through the same
group.

Scope, stated plainly: this lands the mechanism and four implementations
(SRWLOCK on CRITICAL_SECTION, GetTickCount64, InitOnceExecuteOnce, and the
AppUserModelID pair). The other ~29 XP symbols are not written yet, condition
variables being the notable gap. Condition variables need a lazily allocated
wait queue behind a single pointer, the same shape srwlock.cc uses for its
CRITICAL_SECTION. With use_winwrap off -- the default -- none of this is
reached, so the patch is inert by construction.

One thing this cannot do, recorded in redirects.cc so it is not rediscovered
the hard way: the SRWLOCK redirects must not be used on Vista. Vista exports
the family except the two Try variants, and redirecting only those would point
a CRITICAL_SECTION-based Try at a lock whose Ptr field the real implementation
owns. The family is all ours or all the system's.

diff --git a/build/config/BUILD.gn b/build/config/BUILD.gn
--- a/build/config/BUILD.gn
+++ b/build/config/BUILD.gn
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 import("//build/config/rust.gni")
 import("//build/config/sanitizers/sanitizers.gni")
 import("//build/config/ui.gni")
+import("//third_party/winwrap/winwrap.gni")
 if (is_android) {
   import("//build/config/android/abi.gni")
 }
@@ -293,6 +294,22 @@
     # crbug.com/402171653 for context.
     public_deps += [ "//third_party/compiler-rt:atomic" ]
   }
+
+  # Win32 functions the oldest supported Windows does not export. This has to
+  # reach every executable and shared library: the redirect works by satisfying
+  # the __imp_<name> slot a dllimport call site loads through, and every final
+  # link resolves those independently. common_deps is the single place covering
+  # all three link kinds, which is why it goes here and not on any one target.
+  #
+  # Inert unless use_winwrap is set -- winwrap_lib is an empty group then.
+  #
+  # is_win is current_os == "win", not target_os, so this is also what keeps
+  # host-toolchain targets out of it. A cross build from Linux compiles host
+  # tools (mksnapshot, protoc) through this same group, and they must not pull
+  # a library of Win32 implementations.
+  if (use_winwrap && is_win) {
+    public_deps += [ "//third_party/winwrap:winwrap_lib" ]
+  }
 }
 
 # Only the executable template in BUILDCONFIG.gn should reference this.
diff --git a/third_party/winwrap/BUILD.gn b/third_party/winwrap/BUILD.gn
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..23c74925c2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/third_party/winwrap/BUILD.gn
@@ -0,0 +1,91 @@
+# Copyright 2026 Alex313031. All rights reserved.
+# Use of this source code is governed by a BSD-style license that can be
+# found in the LICENSE file.
+
+import("//third_party/winwrap/winwrap.gni")
+
+assert(is_win || target_os == "win", "winwrap only has a reason to exist on Windows")
+
+# The implementations. Shared by both link modes; what differs is how calls are
+# steered into them (see redirects.cc for the static mode, winwrap.def for the
+# shared one).
+_winwrap_sources = [
+  "app_user_model_id.cc",
+  "init_once.cc",
+  "srwlock.cc",
+  "tick_count.cc",
+  "winwrap.h",
+  "winwrap_version.h",
+]
+
+config("winwrap_public_config") {
+  include_dirs = [ "." ]
+}
+
+# winwrap has to run on the oldest target itself, so it gets none of the
+# toolchain's newer-Windows assumptions and may not be instrumented with
+# anything that pulls in a post-XP import of its own.
+config("winwrap_internal_config") {
+  cflags = [
+    "-U_WIN32_WINNT", # Must undef first to avoid redefinition errors.
+    "-D_WIN32_WINNT=0x0501" # XP, for winwrap's own sources only.
+  ]
+  configs = []
+}
+
+shared_library("winwrap_shared") {
+  defines = [ "WINWRAP_DLL" ]
+  output_name = "winwrap"
+  # dll.cc contains functions only a DLL would want.
+  sources = _winwrap_sources + [ "dll.cc", "winwrap.rc" ]
+
+  # //build/config:common_deps depends on winwrap_lib, and every
+  # shared_library picks common_deps up by default -- which would make
+  # winwrap depend on itself. Opting out breaks that cycle. The static form
+  # does not need this, because static_library targets never get
+  # common_deps in the first place.
+  no_default_deps = true
+
+  # Exports the public Win32 names, aliased onto the winwrap_ implementations,
+  # so the generated import library satisfies __imp_<name> and the linker
+  # binds to winwrap.dll instead of kernel32.
+  inputs = [ "winwrap.def" ]
+  ldflags = [ "/DEF:" + rebase_path("winwrap.def", root_build_dir) ]
+
+  configs += [ ":winwrap_internal_config" ]
+  public_configs = [ ":winwrap_public_config" ]
+}
+
+static_library("winwrap_static") {
+  output_name = "winwrap"
+
+  # redirects.cc is what makes the static mode work: it defines the
+  # __imp_<name> slots that dllimport call sites load through, so the linker
+  # resolves them here and never extracts kernel32.lib's member for them.
+  sources = _winwrap_sources + [ "redirects.cc" ]
+
+  configs += [ ":winwrap_internal_config" ]
+  public_configs = [ ":winwrap_public_config" ]
+}
+
+# Depend on this rather than on ":winwrap" directly, so a build with
+# use_winwrap = false links nothing at all and the dependency can stay
+# unconditional at the call site.
+group("winwrap_lib") {
+  if (use_winwrap) {
+    if (is_component_winwrap) {
+      public_deps = [ ":winwrap_shared" ]
+    } else {
+      public_deps = [ ":winwrap_static" ]
+    }
+  }
+}
+
+group("winwrap_all") {
+  testonly = true
+  # Build both static and shared versions of the lib
+  deps = [
+    ":winwrap_static",
+    ":winwrap_shared"
+  ]
+}
